  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: d1cd0062f86f6db42bf8d99878fe6372, type: 3}
  m_Name: HowToPlay
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  data:
  - '<b>Objective:</b> The objective of 8 Ball Pool is to pot your designated balls
    (stripes or solids) and then pot the 8 ball to win the game.


    <b>Setup:</b>
    The game starts with the balls arranged in a triangle with the 8 ball in the
    center.


    <b>1. Break Shot:</b> The player who breaks must strike the cue
    ball with the cue stick, aiming to hit the triangular rack of balls. The break
    should scatter the balls.


    <b>2. Choosing Groups:</b> After the break, the
    first player to pot a ball chooses either the solid balls or the striped balls
    to pot.


    <b>3. Taking Turns:</b> Players take turns trying to pot their
    designated balls. On each turn, the player must strike the cue ball, aiming for
    their designated group.


    <b>4. Potting Balls:</b> If the player pots a ball
    from their group, they continue their turn. If they miss or commit a foul, the
    opponent takes their turn.


    <b>5. Potting the 8 Ball:</b> Once all balls
    from a player''s group are potted, they can attempt to pot the 8 ball. The player
    must call their shot before attempting to pot the 8 ball.


    <b>Winning the
    Game:</b> The game is won by potting the 8 ball after all your designated balls
    have been potted. However, potting the 8 ball prematurely or committing a foul
    when trying to pot the 8 ball results in a loss.


    <b>Fouls:</b> Common fouls
    include scratching the cue ball, potting the cue ball, or hitting the wrong ball
    first.


    <b>Good Luck and Enjoy the Game!</b>'
  - 
  - 
  - "<b>Objective:</b> The objective of Carrom is to pocket all of your designated
    carrom pieces (either white or black) and to successfully pocket and cover the
    queen to win the game.\n\n<b>Setup:</b> The game begins with the pieces arranged
    in a specific pattern at the center of the Carrom board. The queen is placed
    at the center with the remaining pieces positioned symmetrically around it.\n\n<b>1.
    First Strike:</b> A coin toss typically determines who plays first. The starting
    player places the striker on one of the board\u2019s baselines and flicks it
    with their finger, aiming to hit the pieces towards the pockets.\n\n<b>2. Taking
    Turns:</b> Players alternate turns, flicking the striker to pocket their assigned
    pieces. If a player successfully pockets one of their pieces, they earn an additional
    turn. A missed shot or committing a foul ends their turn.\n\n<b>3. The Queen:</b>
    The queen, which is usually the red piece, can be pocketed after a player has
    pocketed at least one of their own pieces. However, to secure the queen, the
    player must \"cover\" it by pocketing another of their pieces in the same turn.
    If the queen is not covered, it is returned to the center of the board.\n\n<b>4.
    Fouls:</b> Common fouls include pocketing the striker, failing to hit any piece,
    or making an illegal move. When a foul is committed, the player typically incurs
    a penalty\u2014often requiring a previously pocketed piece to be returned to
    the board.\n\n<b>5. Winning the Game:</b> The game is won when a player has pocketed
    all of their designated pieces and has successfully pocketed and covered the
    queen. Pocketing the queen prematurely or committing a foul while attempting
    to cover the queen may result in a loss.\n\n<b>Good Luck and Enjoy the Game!</b>"
  - 
  - 
  - '<b>Objective:</b> The goal of the 12 Bead game is to move the beads along the
    path and try to capture the opponent''s beads, while avoiding being captured.


    <b>Setup:</b>
    The game starts with 12 beads placed in a row on the board, with each player
    controlling a set of beads. Players take turns moving their beads.


    <b>1.
    Moving the Beads:</b> On each turn, players move one of their beads along the
    path. The bead can move forward, and depending on the rules, it can jump over
    other beads.


    <b>2. Capturing Beads:</b> A bead is captured when it lands
    on an opponent''s bead, removing it from the board. This can happen if a bead
    jumps over an opponent''s bead in certain directions.


    <b>3. Blocking the
    Opponent:</b> Players can strategically place their beads to block the opponent''s
    movements and prevent them from capturing their own beads.


    <b>4. Winning
    the Game:</b> The game is won when one player successfully captures all of the
    opponent''s beads, or when they reach the final position in the game with the
    most beads remaining.


    <b>Strategy Tips:</b> Think ahead and plan your moves
    carefully. Use your beads to both defend and attack.


    <b>Good Luck and Have
    Fun!</b>'
  - 
  - 
